    ('---\nname: songsee\ndescription: Generate spectrograms and feature-panel visualizations from audio with the songsee CLI.\nhomepage: https://github.com/steipete/songsee\nmetadata:\n  {\n    "openclaw":\n      {\n        "emoji": "🌊",\n        "requires": { "bins": ["songsee"] },\n        "install":\n          [\n            {\n              "id": "brew",\n              "kind": "brew",\n              "formula": "steipete/tap/songsee",\n              "bins": ["songsee"],\n              "label": "Install songsee (brew)",\n            },\n          ],\n      },\n  }\n---\n\n# songsee\n\nGenerate spectrograms + feature panels from audio.\n\nQuick start\n\n- Spectrogram: `songsee track.mp3`\n- Multi-panel: `songsee track.mp3 --viz spectrogram,mel,chroma,hpss,selfsim,loudness,tempogram,mfcc,flux`\n- Time slice: `songsee track.mp3 --start 12.5 --duration 8 -o slice.jpg`\n- Stdin: `cat track.mp3 | songsee - --format png -o out.png`\n\nCommon flags\n\n- `--viz` list (repeatable or comma-separated)\n- `--style` palette (classic, magma, inferno, viridis, gray)\n- `--width` / `--height` output size\n- `--window` / `--hop` FFT settings\n- `--min-freq` / `--max-freq` frequency range\n- `--start` / `--duration` time slice\n- `--format` jpg|png\n\nNotes\n\n- WAV/MP3 decode native; other formats use ffmpeg if available.\n- Multiple `--viz` renders a grid.\n', True),
    # VALID: Antonio-Ardigo/claude-cowork-plugins
    ('---\nname: dionysiac\ndescription: The Dionysiac aesthetic principle in Greek tragedy, as defined by Nietzsche in The Birth of Tragedy. Use when classifying a tragedy\'s structural, thematic, and formal qualities related to dissolution, excess, intoxication, the chorus as collective voice, ecstatic suffering, boundary-violation, sparagmos, and the failure of rational control. CRITICAL -- this is an abstract aesthetic category, NOT a measure of whether Dionysus appears as a character or whether the plot involves wine, revelry, or maenads.\n---\n\n# The Dionysiac Principle\n\nThe Dionysiac (*das Dionysische*) is one of two fundamental aesthetic drives that Nietzsche identifies in Greek art. Named after Dionysus -- god of wine, ecstasy, and ritual dismemberment -- the Dionysiac principle governs **the dissolution of individuation, the eruption of primal unity, and the terrifying wisdom that form is illusion**.\n\nThe Dionysiac does NOT refer to Dionysus\'s literal presence in a play. A tragedy that never mentions Dionysus can be profoundly Dionysiac; a play that stages Dionysiac ritual might handle it in an Apollonian frame. The category is structural and aesthetic, not mythological or thematic-by-content.\n\n## The Spirit Principle\n\n**The Dionysiac is identified by the spirit of the form, not by what the form describes.**\n\nA chorus is Dionysiac not because it speaks *about* ecstasy, madness, or dissolution -- but because **its verse itself becomes ecstatic, incantatory, or formally dissolving**, even when its ostensible subject would not demand it. A speech is Dionysiac not because it reports a dismemberment, but because **the speech itself fragments, overwhelms, or loses its rational structure**.\n\nThe decisive question is never *"Does this element describe Dionysiac things?"* but *"Does this element behave in a Dionysiac way?"*\n\n- A chorus that describes civic order in ecstatic, astrophic, rhythmically overwhelming verse = **Dionysiac in spirit**\n- A chorus that describes divine frenzy in measured, sententious, gnomic stichomythia = **Apollonian in spirit**\n- A messenger who reports a dismemberment in calm, composed, visually precise narrative = **Apollonian in spirit**\n- A hero who discusses a rational plan in language that floods, fragments, and overwhelms its own syntax = **Dionysiac in spirit**\n\nContent is never category. The spirit of the formal element is everything.\n\n## Core Concept: The Shattering of the Principium Individuationis\n\nThe Dionysiac is the **destruction of individuation** -- the experience in which the boundaries between self and world, between one person and another, dissolve. The Dionysiac hero does not maintain identity; identity is stripped away, overwhelmed, or revealed as illusion.\n\n> "Under the charm of the Dionysian not only is the union between man and man reaffirmed, but nature which has become alienated, hostile, or subjugated, celebrates once more her reconciliation with her lost son, man." -- Nietzsche, *The Birth of Tragedy*, sec. 1\n\nThe **Wisdom of Silenus** stands behind the Dionysiac: when King Midas captured Silenus and demanded to know what is best for man, the companion of Dionysus laughed and said: *"The very best thing is utterly beyond your reach: not to have been born, not to be, to be nothing. But the second best thing for you is -- to die soon."* The Dionysiac does not flinch from this abyss; it stares into it.\n\n## Dimensions of the Dionysiac\n\n### 1. Intoxication and Ecstasy (Rausch)\n\nThe Dionysiac is the art of **intoxication** -- not literal drunkenness, but a formal quality in which **the verse, the structure, or the dramatic movement itself behaves as if intoxicated**:\n\n- **Formal intoxication**: The verse exceeds its own rational structure -- syntax floods, repetition builds beyond functional purpose, rhythm overwhelms meaning. The test is not whether a character *describes* an altered state, but whether the *language itself* enters one\n- **Loss of formal control**: The dramatic structure is overwhelmed by forces it cannot contain -- scenes break their expected shape, speeches exceed their argumentative function, the action escapes the plot\'s rational architecture\n- **Ritual patterning**: The dramatic action takes on the quality of ritual repetition, incantation, or compulsion -- not because ritual is *depicted*, but because the form *behaves* ritually\n- **Music over image**: The auditory, rhythmic, incantatory quality of the verse dominates over its visual, representational, or argumentative content\n\n### 2. Dissolution of Boundaries\n\nDionysiac tragedy **formally enacts** the violation of the boundaries that Apollonian order maintains. The test is not whether a character *crosses* a boundary in the plot, but whether **the formal elements themselves refuse to respect the distinction**:\n\n- **Self/other**: Not merely that a character is mistaken for another, but that the verse, the dramatic structure, or the choral voice **makes the distinction between identities formally unstable** -- doubling, mirroring, or collapsing subject positions within the language itself\n- **Human/animal**: Not merely that a transformation is depicted, but that the language **itself becomes bestial, predatory, or hybrid** -- losing its human, civic, rational quality\n- **Male/female**: Not merely that a character cross-dresses, but that the dramatic form **destabilizes gender categories** through its own formal behavior -- lyric and speech modes crossing, active and passive roles inverting\n- **Living/dead**: Not merely that ghosts appear, but that the dramatic present and the mythological past **become formally indistinguishable** -- the dead speak in the present tense of the living, the boundary between memory and action dissolves\n- **Inside/outside**: Not merely that a secret is revealed, but that the dramatic form **exposes interiority** -- the language of inner states erupts into public speech, the house vomits its contents onto the stage\n\n### 3. The Body Violated\n\nThe Apollonian body is dignified and composed. The Dionysiac body is **torn, transformed, or consumed**:\n\n- **Sparagmos**: Ritual dismemberment -- literal or metaphorical tearing apart of the hero\n- **Physical extremity**: Blinding, flaying, mutilation, self-harm visible or described in excess\n- **The body as site of meaning**: Suffering inscribed directly on flesh rather than expressed through speech\n- **Excess beyond measure**: Pain that exceeds what form can contain\n\n### 4. The Dionysiac Chorus\n\nThe chorus is the primary diagnostic instrument for classification. Nietzsche argues that tragedy itself was *born from the chorus* -- from the dithyramb, the ecstatic choral song performed at the festival of Dionysus. The chorus is the original Dionysiac element; the individual hero (the actor) is the Apollonian addition. To analyze the chorus is to ask how much of its dithyrambic origin survives in the finished work.\n\nThe chorus must be analyzed in its actual choral parts -- parodos, stasima, kommos, exodos -- not summarized as a generality.\n\n#### Choral Forms and Their Dionysiac Character\n\n| Choral Part | Definition | Dionysiac Signature |\n|-------------|-----------|---------------------|\n| **Parodos** (entry song) | The chorus\'s first entrance into the orchestra | Emotionally charged entrance; the chorus arrives already overwhelmed, frightened, or ecstatic; the tone is set by feeling, not by social identity |\n| **Stasima** (standing songs) | Choral odes between episodes, typically 3-5 per play | Emotional intensification rather than reflection; mythological parallels that *enact* horror rather than interpret it; imagery of dissolution, blood, darkness; strophic structure strained or broken |\n| **Kommos** (lyric exchange) | Sung dialogue between actor(s) and chorus | The chorus is drawn *into* the hero\'s suffering; the boundary between chorus and actor dissolves; shared lamentation, antiphonal cries, emotional merging |\n| **Exodos** (final passage) | The chorus\'s closing words or departure | No tidy moral; the chorus is left shattered, bewildered, or reduced to bare lamentation; the closing is a wound, not a bandage |\n| **Astrophic passages** | Lyric passages that break the strophe/antistrophe pattern | The formal balance of strophe/antistrophe gives way to irregular, unpaired lyric -- a signal that metrical order itself is dissolving |\n\n#### How the Dionysiac Chorus Functions\n\n- **Participates and enacts** rather than observes -- the chorus dances, moves, embodies the action\n- **Loses its social identity**: The chorus ceases to function within its assigned social role and becomes the voice of something larger -- the community, the earth, the primal collective\n- **Emotional saturation**: Lyric passages of overwhelming intensity -- lamentation, ecstatic hymn, incantatory repetition\n- **Ritual presence**: The chorus recalls its own origin in the dithyramb; it functions as a ritual body, not merely a dramatic device\n- **Splits or fragments**: The chorus may divide, argue with itself, or lose its unified voice -- internal dissolution mirroring the hero\'s\n- **Drives the emotional arc**: The stasima do not comment on the action from outside; they *are* the emotional core of the play\n\n#### Abstract Traits of Dionysiac Chorus Composition\n\nThe chorus signals the Dionysiac not through *who it is* (its mythological or social identity) but through **how its collective identity functions aesthetically**:\n\n- **Disrupted social position**: The chorus\'s members have been displaced from a stable social order -- their identity is defined by loss, exile, or subjugation rather than by civic role\n- **Non-human or liminal nature**: The chorus exists outside the normal categories of civic life -- it is not bound by the social order that structures Apollonian tragedy\n- **Emotional rather than institutional definition**: The chorus is defined by a shared emotional or experiential state (grief, terror, ecstasy) rather than by a social position (rank, profession, citizenship)\n- **Collective overwhelm**: The chorus\'s identity as a group matters less than its function as a vessel for forces that exceed individual or institutional containment\n\nWhen the chorus loses its defined social role, merges with the hero\'s suffering, breaks its metrical structure, or becomes a ritual presence rather than a civic commentator, it functions as the Dionysiac heart of the tragedy.\n\n### 5. Irreconcilable Contradiction\n\nDionysiac tragedy refuses the comfort of resolution:\n\n- **No moral clarity**: Both sides of the conflict may be right, or both wrong, or the categories themselves collapse\n- **Suffering without redemption**: Pain does not produce wisdom -- or the "wisdom" it produces is unbearable (Silenus)\n- **The absurd**: Events that resist rational explanation, divine actions that seem arbitrary or cruel\n- **Open wounds**: The ending does not heal; it leaves the audience in the space of unresolved horror\n\n### 6. The Primal Unity (Ur-Eines)\n\nBehind the Dionysiac lies Nietzsche\'s metaphysical claim: beneath the world of individual forms lies a **primal unity** -- an undifferentiated ground of being that is both creative and destructive:\n\n- **Return to origin**: The Dionysiac moment is a return to this primal ground\n- **Creation through destruction**: New forms emerge only when old ones are shattered\n- **The eternal return**: What is destroyed comes back -- the god himself is torn apart and reborn\n- **Tragic joy**: The paradox that the most devastating tragedies produce not despair but a strange exhilaration\n\n## Diagnostic Markers\n\nWhen analyzing a tragedy for Dionysiac qualities, look for **how the formal elements behave**, not what they describe:\n\n| Marker | Dionysiac Signal (formal behavior) |\n|--------|-------------------------------------|\n| **Hero\'s speech** | The hero\'s language itself becomes unstable -- syntax fragments, rhetorical structure dissolves, the speech loses its deliberative or forensic quality |\n| **Suffering\'s formal expression** | The verse that expresses suffering exceeds its own containment -- language overflows, repetition builds beyond function, the form cannot hold the content |\n| **How knowledge arrives** | Knowledge does not arrive through argument or sight but through formal eruption -- the truth breaks into the structure rather than being deduced within it |\n| **The ending\'s formal quality** | The dramatic structure refuses to close cleanly -- the ending feels forced, hollow, or formally unresolved, regardless of whether the plot is "resolved" |\n| **The chorus\'s verse** | The choral verse itself becomes ecstatic, incantatory, or astrophic -- the meter breaks, the strophic balance strains, the language overwhelms its own subject |\n| **Language register** | The language exceeds its argumentative or descriptive function -- rhythm, sound, and repetition dominate over meaning and logic |\n| **How bodies are spoken of** | The language that describes the body becomes formally excessive -- the narration of physical events strains beyond composure |\n| **Formal boundary-crossing** | Dramatic modes bleed into each other -- lyric invades speech, speech becomes song, the chorus enters the actor\'s space, episode and stasimon lose their formal separation |\n\n## Common Misconceptions\n\n| Misconception | Correction |\n|---------------|------------|\n| A play is Dionysiac because Dionysus appears in it | A tragedy featuring Dionysus as a character is Dionysiac only if it *enacts* the dissolution of individuation, sparagmos, and the failure of rational control. A play about Dionysus told as a neat moral fable would be Apollonian |\n| Dionysiac means "chaotic" or "badly structured" | Dionysiac tragedy can be formally sophisticated. The dissolution is thematic and experiential, not a failure of craft |\n| Dionysiac means "about wine and partying" | The intoxication is metaphysical -- the dissolution of the boundaries of selfhood. A sober play about a king losing his identity is more Dionysiac than a comedy about drinking |\n| Dionysiac is inferior to Apollonian | Nietzsche argues the opposite -- that the greatest art emerges when the Dionysiac threatens to overwhelm Apollonian form, and form barely holds. The Dionysiac is the deeper truth; the Apollonian is the necessary veil |\n| Any violence makes a play Dionysiac | Apollonian tragedy can depict violence -- but it frames violence through formally composed narration (the messenger speech) that maintains measure. Dionysiac violence is marked by the *narration itself* losing its composure, not by the violence being depicted |\n| The presence of satyrs makes a play Dionysiac | Satyrs are mythological figures associated with Dionysus, but their presence is content, not category. A play featuring satyrs in a comic, orderly frame is Apollonian in aesthetic character. Classification rests on structural and formal qualities, never on mythological personnel |\n| A play is Dionysiac because it depicts ecstasy, madness, or frenzy | **This is the Content Fallacy.** A play that *describes* ecstasy in measured, gnomic, formally balanced verse is Apollonian in spirit. A play is Dionysiac only when the **form itself** becomes ecstatic -- when the verse behaves as intoxicated language regardless of its subject |\n\n## Recognizing Dionysiac Tendencies\n\nA tragedy leans Dionysiac when:\n\n- The hero\'s identity **dissolves** -- they become simultaneously who they are and their own opposite, or their self-knowledge annihilates rather than redeems\n- **Sparagmos** (literal or metaphorical dismemberment) drives the climax -- the bounded self is torn apart\n- **Madness, possession, or divine frenzy** overwhelms rational agency, and the hero cannot master events through deliberation\n- A **blood-curse or ritual pollution** saturates the house, the family, the city -- contamination that resists civic or legal resolution\n- Every **category boundary** (Greek/barbarian, mother/destroyer, human/divine, male/female) is transgressed by the action itself\n\nThese are tendencies, not absolute categories. Most tragedies contain both impulses, and the classification operates on a spectrum.\n', True),
